course: dictionary
lesson: 6714
title: Offensive: hurtful words and attacks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words hurt people, even when the person saying them does not know what they mean. «Some words are hurtful or offensive even if the child saying them does not know their meaning.» So offensive can describe a word that is rude or insulting, because it upsets people.
explain: Offensive can also describe something very unpleasant to the senses, such as a bad smell. Read the words around it to find which meaning fits. Ask: does the sentence talk about upsetting people, a smell, sport, or a war?
explain: Look for clue words. «No offence, but» comes just before something offensive, so the speaker is warning you that what follows may upset you. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Offensive means rude or insulting, likely to upset people.
fact: Offensive can also mean very unpleasant to the senses, like an offensive smell.
fact: In sports such as American football and basketball, offensive means to do with attacking and scoring, not defending.
fact: An offensive is a big attack by an army in a war, as in «launch an offensive».
fact: «Go on the offensive» means to start attacking.
exercise: «Some words are hurtful or offensive even if the child saying them does not know their meaning.» What does offensive mean here? | Written down in a book | Very large and heavy | *Rude or insulting, likely to upset people | Funny and playful
exercise: «The room had an offensive smell of old rubbish.» What does offensive mean in this sentence? | *Very unpleasant to the senses | Kind and friendly | Bright and shiny
exercise: In American football, what is the offensive team's job? | Defending its own goal | *Attacking and scoring | Handing out the tickets
test: «No offence, but your drawing looks like a potato.» What does «No offence, but» warn you about? | A question about dinner | *Something that may upset the listener | A surprise present
test: Which phrase means to start attacking? | Go on the stairs | Go on the bus | *Go on the offensive | Go on the holiday
exam: In a war, what is an offensive? | A type of uniform | A peace meeting | *A large attack by an army | A quiet river
